Raynaud's Phenomenon
Cardiovascular
Episodic digital colour changes, Cold/stress precipitation, Pallor/cyanosis/rubor sequence, Normal peripheral pulses

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe throbbing pain and sudden, frightening discoloration of the fingers.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe presentation of painful, blanched, and cyanotic digits. In an after-hours setting, this presentation raises immediate concern for acute embolic arterial occlusion or critical digital ischemia. Urgent in-person physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate peripheral perfusion, rule out an acute surgical emergency, and provide immediate symptomatic relief, precluding any safe delay until routine in-hours care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My fingers suddenly went completely dead and white in the cold, and now they are throbbing with an agonizing, burning pain as they turn bright red."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced one hour ago after the patient was exposed to cold evening temperatures while retrieving mail. The patient experienced episodic, sharply demarcated color changes in the digits of both hands. This followed a sequential triphasic color response: extreme pallor (white), progressing to cyanosis (blue), and currently entering intense rubor (red). The hyperemic recovery phase is accompanied by intense pain, throbbing, and severe paresthesia. Prior to this, the patient experienced a profound numbness or "dead finger" sensation during the ischemic phase. KEY SYMPTOMS: Episodic sharply demarcated colour changes in the digits of the hands or feet. Sequential triphasic colour response consisting of extreme pallor, cyanosis, and subsequent intense rubor. Precipitation of attacks by cold exposure or emotional stress. Intense pain, throbbing, or paraesthesia during the hyperaemic recovery phase. Numbness or 'dead finger' sensation during the ischaemic phase.
Functional Status: Currently unable to use hands for fine motor tasks due to severe throbbing pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute distress regarding the sudden visual changes in the hands.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Anxious, rubbing hands together vigorously, in moderate discomfort.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Completely normal radial and ulnar pulses bilaterally with strong upstrokes. Extremities: Symmetrical and bilateral involvement. Digits of both hands are currently in the hyperemic phase (intensely red and warm to touch), resolving from earlier pallor. Complete absence of tissue necrosis, ulceration, or gangrene. Capillary refill is currently brisk (<2 seconds). DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Episodic digital colour changes, Cold/stress precipitation, Pallor/cyanosis/rubor sequence, Normal peripheral pulses.
Environmental Observations: House temperature is notably low; patient is inadequately dressed for the ambient temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently commenced a beta-blocker for migraine prophylaxis, which frequently worsens symptoms of peripheral vasospasm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Raynaud's Phenomenon - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Attack)
Differentials:

Acute Arterial Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral involvement rather than a single ischemic limb, combined with completely normal, bounding radial and ulnar pulses.

Buerger's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of tissue necrosis or gangrene, absence of heavy tobacco smoking history, and the transient, reversible triphasic nature of the attack.

Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, visible, sharply demarcated color changes corresponding exactly with the onset and offset of the sensory symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic, severe triphasic color response (pallor, cyanosis, rubor) of Raynaud's Phenomenon, precipitated by cold exposure and exacerbated by recent beta-blocker initiation. While ultimately a benign vasospastic condition, the sudden onset of painful, white, numb digits presented a clinical picture indistinguishable by phone from acute arterial occlusion. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to palpate strong peripheral pulses, categorically rule out a limb-threatening embolic event, and safely manage the severe hyperemic pain without requiring an emergency hospital transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to immediately cease the recently prescribed beta-blocker (pending GP review). Administered simple oral analgesia (Paracetamol/Ibuprofen) for the hyperemic throbbing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to physically place their hands under warm (not hot) running water or into their armpits at the very first sign of finger pallor. By directly applying gentle, active warming before the cyanotic phase sets in, the patient aborts the severe vasospasm early. The reward is entirely bypassing the intensely painful, throbbing hyperemic recovery phase, reinforcing early intervention.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Advised the immediate purchase and use of insulated gloves for all cold exposure.
Follow-up: Routine General Practice review in 3-5 days to discuss alternative migraine prophylaxis and screen for secondary rheumatological causes (e.g., ANA testing).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If a finger remains completely white and numb for more than one hour despite active warming, or if any skin begins to turn black or develop sores, the patient must attend the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; secondary underlying autoimmune causes may require formal blood testing; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Primary Raynaud's is a lifelong but manageable condition consisting of hyper-reactive blood vessels. Preventing attacks via thermal protection is the mainstay of treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your core and hands warm at all times. Use warm water to abort attacks. Seek emergency care immediately if a finger remains permanently white or numb.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The small blood vessels in your fingers are overreacting to the cold and clamping shut, cutting off the blood supply. When they finally relax, blood rushes back in, causing the intense redness and throbbing pain. The beta-blocker you started recently makes this clamping action worse.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the beta-blocker. Wear gloves whenever opening the freezer or going outside in the cold.

